---
import AppCTA from '../components/AppCTA.astro';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { vocab, units } from '../lib/learn';
import { siteUrl } from '../lib/site';

const title = 'How the Bol Marathi Curriculum Works';
const description = 'Where Bol Marathi content comes from: app curriculum as source of truth, auditable sync to this site, and language QA tracking.';
---

<Base title={title} description={description}>
	<div class="prose">
		<p class="kicker">Methodology</p>
		<h1>How the curriculum works</h1>
		<p class="lede">
			One source of truth, two products. The website never invents words —
			everything teachable here comes from the app's curriculum.
		</p>
		<h2>1. Source: the app curriculum</h2>
		<p>
			{units.length} unit files × 6 lessons ({units.length * 6} lessons) with
			grammar, culture, speaking, listening, Hindi→Marathi bridges and
			sentence builders — plus a {vocab.length}-word trilingual vocabulary
			with transliteration and examples.
		</p>
		<h2>2. Sync: auditable, repeatable</h2>
		<p>
			A zero-dependency script copies the curriculum into versioned snapshots.
			Every website update is a reviewable diff — nothing is hand-copied,
			nothing silently rewritten.
		</p>
		<h2>3. QA: flagged, not faked</h2>
		<p>
			Automated scans check every entry for missing fields, duplicates and
			script errors; samples are reviewed by hand. Uncertain Marathi is
			flagged for native review rather than "improved" by guesswork.
		</p>
		<h2>4. Website vs app</h2>
		<p>
			The website teaches reading knowledge: discover, understand, read.
			The app adds the practice loop: listen, speak, build, review,
			remember, track. Static pages never pretend to be interactive lessons.
		</p>
		<p><em>Curated by Bol Marathi.</em></p>
		<AppCTA variant="general" context="method" />
	</div>
</Base>
